6. Theft of the Moon. In the past people believed that a lunar eclipse happens because a huge celestial serpent (dragon) swallows the Moon. Incidentally, this is why the period between two passages of the Moon through the node of its orbit is called the "draconic month". In Bulgaria, during a lunar eclipse, men went out into the forest and shot at the Moon to frighten the serpent and make it return the Moon. What will happen if the serpent gets tired of all this and next time, when a lunar eclipse is due according to its plan, it swallows the Moon and instantly flies away with it for ever – how will the orbit of the Earth around the Sun and the period of its orbital motion change? First answer the question qualitatively, and then estimate quantitatively. The original orbits may be considered circular.

Note. The quantitative solution of the problem (at least the author's one) will require quite a few algebraic transformations. To simplify the calculations (so as not to get confused and not to confuse the jury), we recommend using ratios, denoting by:

$a$ – the ratio of the Earth–Moon distance to the Earth–Sun distance;

$p$ – the ratio of the masses of the Moon and the Earth;

$Y$ – the ratio of the period of revolution of the Moon around the Earth to the period of revolution of the Earth around the Sun.

Also, so as not to get confused, we do not recommend doing the calculations straight away. Moreover, the algebraic answer turns out to be very interestingly filled with physical meaning.

XI.7. A distant object

You see a map of the apparent path among the stars of a Kuiper belt object near its perihelion over several years. The upward direction corresponds to the direction to the north pole of the ecliptic; the scale of the map and the dates of the beginning and end of the track are indicated. The intervals between neighbouring marks on the track correspond to 20 days. Determine from this map:

1) the distance of the object from the Sun;

2) its orbital period;

3) the constellation in which the object is located.

